<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alcázar of Seville</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Royal Alcázar of Seville</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alcázar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> is a royal palace in Seville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, developed from an Islamic fortress of the Abbadid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> periods. After the Castilian conquest of 1248 the kings rebuilt it, and Peter I's palace of the 1360s, decorated by Muslim craftsmen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, is a masterpiece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjar</a> art. It is still used by the Spanish royal family and is a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alcazar-of-seville.jpg" alt="Alcázar of Seville" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ajay Suresh from New York, NY, USA. Edited by MenkinAlRire · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sevilla-2-2_(48040287512)-edit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
